%I A402181 #15 Nov 22 2026 21:19:44
%S A402181 6,85,176,194,200,375,1517,1523,3042,5953,6043,6109,12068,12104,96251,
%T A402181 193734,386797,387589
%N A402181 Numbers k such that the decimal concatenation of the sum and product of the digits of 2^k, in either order (allowing leading zeros), is a power of 2.
%C A402181 Assuming 2^86 is the largest power of 2 without a zero digit (A007377), every term after k = 6 is a number k whose digit sum of 2^k is a power of 2 (A063565).
%C A402181 Using A095412, the following are also terms: 1545477, 3092224, 3098800, 6188717, 6191693, 6199469, 24753865, 99084345. - _Michael S. Branicky_, Nov 16 2026
%F A402181 a(n) = A095412(n+5) for n >= 2 (conjectured). - _Michael S. Branicky_, Nov 16 2026
%e A402181 For k = 6, 2^6 = 64 has digit sum 10 and digit product 24, and their concatenation is 1024 = 2^10.
%e A402181 For all other known terms (k >= 85), 2^k contains at least one 0 digit, making the digit product 0. Concatenating 0 followed by the digit sum yields a leading-zero string whose numerical value equals the digit sum itself (for example, at k = 85, 0 || 128 = 128 = 2^7).
%e A402181 85 is a term because 2^85 = 38685626227668133590597632 has digit sum 128 and digit product 0, and concatenating 0 and 128 yields 0128 = 128 = 2^7.
%o A402181 (PARI)
%o A402181 ispow2(n) = n > 0 && hammingweight(n) == 1;
%o A402181 isok(k) = my(d = digits(2^k), s = vecsum(d), p = vecprod(d)); ispow2(eval(Str(s, p))) || ispow2(eval(Str(p, s)));
%o A402181 (Python)
%o A402181 from math import prod
%o A402181 def is_pow2(n): return n > 0 and (n & (n - 1)) == 0
%o A402181 def ok(k):
%o A402181     digits = [int(d) for d in str(1 << k)]
%o A402181     s, p = sum(digits), prod(digits)
%o A402181     return is_pow2(int(f"{s}{p}")) or is_pow2(int(f"{p}{s}"))
%o A402181 print([k for k in range(10001) if ok(k)])
%Y A402181 Cf. A000079, A001370, A007377, A063565, A095412.
%K A402181 nonn,base,hard,more,less,new
%O A402181 1,1
%A A402181 _Dean Grayson_, Nov 14 2026
